WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.250
Planning means deciding what needs to happen before promising a result.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:12.258
Task decomposition means breaking one larger request into small questions or actions.

00:00:12.258 --> 00:00:16.111
A useful step has an outcome you can actually check.

00:00:16.111 --> 00:00:20.903
Imagine trying to book a table before learning how many people will come.

00:00:20.903 --> 00:00:27.709
Some tasks belong in a certain order because the first result determines what you must ask next.

00:00:27.709 --> 00:00:30.234
Treat the plan as a working list.

00:00:30.234 --> 00:00:34.205
As results arrive, keep the steps that still make sense.

00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:40.360
Replace any step that relied on an assumption the new information has disproved.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.141
Ana asks Orbit to swap her tent for a larger one.

00:00:44.141 --> 00:00:52.656
Scout could reply yes immediately, but that assumes the shop knows which tent she owns and has a larger one available.

00:00:52.656 --> 00:01:01.693
Write a short plan: find her order, identify the larger model, check its stock, and only then decide what to offer.

00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:04.757
Does every request need a plan? No.

00:01:04.757 --> 00:01:10.861
For a simple question, like where's my parcel, Scout can decide one step at a time.

00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:17.218
That's quick, but on a long request an agent can wander, or forget part of what was asked.

00:01:17.218 --> 00:01:21.872
For multi-step requests, a brief plan up front keeps it on track.

00:01:21.872 --> 00:01:28.849
For the shop assistant, a short plan gives the software a sequence it can check before using tools.

00:01:28.849 --> 00:01:34.311
And the plan doubles as a record you can read when something goes wrong.

00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:36.459
The order lookup succeeds.

00:01:36.459 --> 00:01:41.614
Scout checks inventory for the larger tent and finds zero available.

00:01:41.614 --> 00:01:43.676
Walk back through the steps.

00:01:43.676 --> 00:01:46.392
Her original order is still valid.

00:01:46.392 --> 00:01:50.704
The stock check also worked; zero is a useful answer.

00:01:50.704 --> 00:01:56.037
Only the planned offer of an immediate swap has become impossible.

00:01:56.037 --> 00:01:59.317
Lay out the proposed exchange as a sequence.

00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:02.234
First identify the tent Ana bought.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:10.010
The next check refers to a larger tent, so make sure it is the right replacement model before checking the warehouse.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.673
Now replace the expected stock count with zero.

00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:16.581
Which part of the work must you throw away?

00:02:16.581 --> 00:02:19.050
The order lookup is still useful.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.107
The stock result is also trustworthy.

00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:25.822
It is the planned offer that no longer makes sense.

00:02:25.822 --> 00:02:31.540
Cross out only the unavailable exchange and explain the remaining choices.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:42.333
A plan that can change one step is more useful than a script which starts the entire conversation again whenever the world differs from its first guess.

00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:48.193
Now the real run. Sam's stock sheet says the three-person tent is sold out.

00:02:48.193 --> 00:02:53.039
Step one works: Ana's two-person tent is already on its way.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:58.332
Step two comes back with zero. There's no three-person tent to swap in.

00:02:58.332 --> 00:03:03.486
Step three no longer makes sense, so Scout re-plans with what it now knows.

00:03:03.486 --> 00:03:12.718
It explains the problem and offers real options: keep the tent that's coming, or return it within thirty days of delivery.

00:03:12.718 --> 00:03:19.493
That beats both bad extremes: promising a swap that can't happen, or simply giving up.

00:03:19.493 --> 00:03:29.458
Scout can explain the shortage and offer the choices supported by the shop’s policy, such as keeping the current tent or discussing a return.

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:34.464
It should not keep running the same stock check hoping for a different answer.

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:41.120
A good plan lets one piece of evidence change exactly the decision that depends on it.
